<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Analyze Underlying Relationship in a Graph of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01405</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Analyze Underlying Relationship in a Graph of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e7fbe1e5-be85-4f47-9c54-41d96f634100</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The cost, in dollars, to produce 1 watt of solar energy is a function of
        the number of years since 1977, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">From 1977 to 1987, the cost could be modeled by an exponential function <m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>. Here is the graph of the function.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
        <media alt="A sheet of solar panels.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_11_03_01.jpg" width="300" />
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00004">
        <media
          alt="Line graph showing a sharp decline in dollars per watt over 12 years since 1977, dropping from about $80 at the y-intercept to below $5 per watt. The curve smooths as it approaches the lower values.">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_11_03_02.jpg" width="300" />
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">1. What is the statement <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>≈</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> saying about this
            situation?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007">9 years after 1977 (or in 1986), the cost in dollars for one watt of
            solar energy was about $6.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">2. What is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? What
            about <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3.5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? What do
            these values represent in this context?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>≈</m:mo>
                <m:mn>25</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, and <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3.5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>≈</m:mo>
                <m:mn>30</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. These values represent
            the cost in dollars of 1 watt of solar energy in 1981 (4 years after 1977) and halfway
            through 1980 (3.5 years after 1977).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">3. When <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>45</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, what is <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>? What does that value of <m:math
              display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> represent in this
            context?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00013">2 years after 1977 (in 1979), the cost in dollars of 1 watt of solar
            energy was $45.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">4. By what factor did the cost of 1 watt of solar energy change each
            year? (If you get stuck, consider creating a table.)</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00016"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            or 0.75</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Understanding Characteristics of Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00017">Watch the following video to learn more about the characteristics of
        graphs:</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4"
          src="https://openstax.org/r/graph-characteristics" />
      </media>
      <para id="para-00018">An imaginary line that a function value will not touch or cross is
        called an <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">asymptote</term>.</para>
      <para id="para-00019">The value of the range that the function will never touch is called the <term
          class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">horizontal asymptote</term>.</para>
      <para id="para-00020">In the function graphed, the horizontal asymptote is <m:math
          display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>0</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00021"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_9_2_SC" /></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
        <para id="para-00023">The graph below describes the amount of caffeine, <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, in a person’s body <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> hours after an initial
          measurement of 100 mg.</para>
        <para id="para-00024">
          <media
            alt="A graph showing caffeine (mg) decreasing over time (hours); caffeine starts at 100 mg and gradually declines as time increases from 0 to 14 hours. A point P is marked on the curve.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_11_05_01.svg" />
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00025">What does the point <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>35</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> mean?</para>
        <para id="para-00026">After 10 hours, there is 35 mg of caffeine in a person’s body.</para>
        <para id="para-00027">What is the value of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> when <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>80</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>?</para>
        <para id="para-00028">When the caffeine concentration is 80 mg, time is 2 hours, so <m:math
            display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
            <problem id="prob-00005">
              <para id="para-00029">Using the graph above, what is the value of <m:math
                  display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          and what does it mean in the context of the situation?</para>
              <para id="para-00030"></para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00005">
              <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00032">Here is how to interpret this exponential graph:</para>
              <para id="para-00033">When <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, the curve
          crosses at 60, so <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>60</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>.</para>
              <para id="para-00034">This means that after 5 hours, the person has 60 mg of caffeine
          in their body.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>